<section class="aq-section aq-faq-section"><div class="aq-in aq-split-section"><div class="aq-split-section__head"><header class="aq-sec-head"><h2 class="aq-h1">Questions engineers ask</h2></header><div class="aq-faq__dial" aria-hidden="true" data-total="05"><span class="aq-faq__dial-n"><span>05</span></span><span class="aq-faq__dial-of"><span class="aq-faq__dial-k">questions</span></span></div></div><div class="aq-split-section__body"><div class="aq-faq"><details name="faq-1"><summary><span class="aq-index" aria-hidden="true">01</span><span class="aq-faq__q">Single-pass or double-pass RO?</span><span class="aq-faq__sign" aria-hidden="true"><span class="aq-faq__glyph"></span></span></summary><div class="aq-faq__a"><p>Single-pass reverse osmosis (RO) with electrodeionization (EDI) suits feed with moderate dissolved solids. Double pass adds margin on conductivity, total organic carbon and silica for high-solids feed.</p></div></details><details name="faq-1"><summary><span class="aq-index" aria-hidden="true">02</span><span class="aq-faq__q">How do you handle cold winter feed?</span><span class="aq-faq__sign" aria-hidden="true"><span class="aq-faq__glyph"></span></span></summary><div class="aq-faq__a"><p>Rated at 25 °C, reverse osmosis (RO) can lose a third or more of its output at 5 °C. We size at your coldest feed, or add a heat exchanger.</p></div></details><details name="faq-1"><summary><span class="aq-index" aria-hidden="true">03</span><span class="aq-faq__q">Our supply is chloraminated. What changes?</span><span class="aq-faq__sign" aria-hidden="true"><span class="aq-faq__glyph"></span></span></summary><div class="aq-faq__a"><p>Chloramine damages reverse osmosis (RO) membranes, so it is removed first, which releases ammonia. Ammonia can pass RO when pH is raised for CO₂ control, so we design both together.</p></div></details><details name="faq-1"><summary><span class="aq-index" aria-hidden="true">04</span><span class="aq-faq__q">How is the PP skid sanitized?</span><span class="aq-faq__sign" aria-hidden="true"><span class="aq-faq__glyph"></span></span></summary><div class="aq-faq__a"><p><span class="aq-ph">[sanitization method for the PP skid]</span>; <span class="aq-ph">[heat-sanitizable RO and EDI, yes or no]</span>. After any chemical sanitization, removal of the sanitant is proven before the water is used (WHO TRS 1033).</p></div></details><details name="faq-1"><summary><span class="aq-index" aria-hidden="true">05</span><span class="aq-faq__q">How do we know it will pass IQ/OQ?</span><span class="aq-faq__sign" aria-hidden="true"><span class="aq-faq__glyph"></span></span></summary><div class="aq-faq__a"><p>The factory acceptance test (FAT) proves function before shipping. The site acceptance test (SAT), then installation and operational qualification (IQ/OQ), prove it on your site and feed water. Your quality unit approves the FAT protocol and can witness it once <span class="aq-ph">[shop and test bay ready for visits]</span>.</p><p class="aq-faq__more"><a class="aq-btn aq-btn--link" href="#svc-fat-sat" data-wp="svc-fat-sat">See what FAT proves <span class="aq-btn__arrow">→</span></a></p></div></details></div></div></div></section>
</div>
